; S1a.3.1 — a nested `(or …)` in a `:match` premise.
;
; An `(or …)` is a whole `:match`: the compiler lowers a top-level one to a
; plan per disjunct. Until S1.22.0 a nested one returned `[]`, which was
; silently wrong in BOTH polarities — this rule fired with neither p nor q in
; the KB (unsound), and its mirror `(absent (or (p ?x) (q ?x)))` never fired
; with neither present (incomplete, and permanently: the empty sub-plan makes
; the guard fail, and a failing monotone guard retires its candidate).
;
; Compile-negative until M1f S1f.5.21 (W62, the close review's finding 1): the
; compiler refused it where it compiled the rule, in the Python IR's spelling,
; `nested `(or …)` in a `:match` premise: SForm(head=Atom(name='or'), …)`,
; `ein saturate` behind `ein.inference.compile.CompileError:`, while the views
; and `kb save` answered. Since then the loader refuses it, one line on every
; command, and this file moved here from `examples/broken/compile/`.
(relation a Thing :why "a holds of {?1}")
(relation p Thing :why "p holds of {?1}")
(relation q Thing :why "q holds of {?1}")

(rule nested-disjunction ()
  :match  (and (a ?x) (or (p ?x) (q ?x)))
  :assert (p ?x) :why "a holds of {?x} and so does p or q, so p holds of {?x}")
